Automating Grafana Dashboard Provisioning with Terraform
Learning Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Install and configure Terraform on a Linux system
Set up Grafana server with proper authentication
Use Terraform to automate Grafana dashboard creation
Manage Grafana data sources through Infrastructure as Code
Configure Grafana settings and organizations using Terraform
Implement version control for Grafana configurations
Troubleshoot common Terraform and Grafana integration issues
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Linux command line operations
Familiarity with JSON and HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language) syntax
Basic knowledge of monitoring concepts and dashboards
Understanding of Infrastructure as Code principles
Experience with text editors like nano or vim
Lab Environment
Al Nafi provides Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your dedicated environment. The provided Linux machine is bare metal with no pre-installed tools, so you will install all required software during the lab exercises.

Task 1: Environment Setup and Tool Installation
Subtask 1.1: Update System and Install Dependencies
First, update your system and install essential packages:

sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt install -y curl wget unzip software-properties-common apt-transport-https ca-certificates gnupg lsb-release
Subtask 1.2: Install Terraform
Download and install the latest version of Terraform:

# Download Terraform
wget https://releases.hashicorp.com/terraform/1.6.6/terraform_1.6.6_linux_amd64.zip

# Unzip and install
unzip terraform_1.6.6_linux_amd64.zip
sudo mv terraform /usr/local/bin/

# Verify installation
terraform version
Subtask 1.3: Install Docker for Grafana
Install Docker to run Grafana in a container:

# Install Docker
curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com -o get-docker.sh
sudo sh get-docker.sh

# Add user to docker group
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER

# Start Docker service
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo systemctl enable docker

# Verify Docker installation
sudo docker --version
Subtask 1.4: Start Grafana Container
Launch Grafana using Docker:

# Create a directory for Grafana data
mkdir -p ~/grafana-data

# Run Grafana container
sudo docker run -d \
  --name grafana \
  -p 3000:3000 \
  -v ~/grafana-data:/var/lib/grafana \
  -e "GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin123" \
  -e "GF_USERS_ALLOW_SIGN_UP=false" \
  grafana/grafana:latest

# Verify Grafana is running
sudo docker ps
Subtask 1.5: Verify Grafana Access
Test Grafana accessibility:

# Wait for Grafana to start (about 30 seconds)
sleep 30

# Test Grafana endpoint
curl -I http://localhost:3000

# Get Grafana container logs to ensure it's running properly
sudo docker logs grafana
Task 2: Configure Terraform for Grafana Integration
Subtask 2.1: Create Terraform Project Structure
Set up the project directory structure:

# Create project directory
mkdir ~/terraform-grafana-lab
cd ~/terraform-grafana-lab

# Create subdirectories
mkdir -p {dashboards,datasources,terraform}
Subtask 2.2: Create Terraform Provider Configuration
Create the main Terraform configuration file:

cat > terraform/main.tf << 'EOF'
terraform {
  required_providers {
    grafana = {
      source  = "grafana/grafana"
      version = "~> 2.9.0"
    }
  }
}

# Configure the Grafana Provider
provider "grafana" {
  url  = "http://localhost:3000"
  auth = "admin:admin123"
}

# Create an organization
resource "grafana_organization" "lab_org" {
  name         = "Lab Organization"
  admin_user   = "admin"
  create_users = true
  admins       = ["admin"]
}

# Create a folder for organizing dashboards
resource "grafana_folder" "monitoring_folder" {
  title = "Monitoring Dashboards"
  org_id = grafana_organization.lab_org.id
}
EOF
Subtask 2.3: Initialize Terraform
Initialize the Terraform working directory:

cd terraform
terraform init
Subtask 2.4: Create Terraform Variables File
Create a variables file for better configuration management:

cat > variables.tf << 'EOF'
variable "grafana_url" {
  description = "Grafana server URL"
  type        = string
  default     = "http://localhost:3000"
}

variable "grafana_admin_password" {
  description = "Grafana admin password"
  type        = string
  default     = "admin123"
  sensitive   = true
}

variable "organization_name" {
  description = "Name of the Grafana organization"
  type        = string
  default     = "Lab Organization"
}
EOF
Task 3: Create and Manage Data Sources with Terraform
Subtask 3.1: Install Prometheus for Data Source
Install Prometheus to serve as a data source:

# Download Prometheus
cd ~
wget https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/releases/download/v2.48.0/prometheus-2.48.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz

# Extract and setup
tar xvfz prometheus-2.48.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz
cd prometheus-2.48.0.linux-amd64

# Create Prometheus configuration
cat > prometheus.yml << 'EOF'
global:
  scrape_interval: 15s

scrape_configs:
  - job_name: 'prometheus'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9090']
  
  - job_name: 'node'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9100']
EOF

# Start Prometheus in background
nohup ./prometheus --config.file=prometheus.yml --storage.tsdb.path=./data > prometheus.log 2>&1 &

# Verify Prometheus is running
sleep 10
curl http://localhost:9090/api/v1/status/config
Subtask 3.2: Create Data Source Configuration
Add Prometheus data source configuration to Terraform:

cd ~/terraform-grafana-lab/terraform

cat >> main.tf << 'EOF'

# Create Prometheus data source
resource "grafana_data_source" "prometheus" {
  type          = "prometheus"
  name          = "Prometheus"
  url           = "http://localhost:9090"
  is_default    = true
  org_id        = grafana_organization.lab_org.id

  json_data_encoded = jsonencode({
    httpMethod   = "POST"
    queryTimeout = "60s"
  })
}

# Create TestData data source for demo purposes
resource "grafana_data_source" "testdata" {
  type       = "testdata"
  name       = "TestData"
  org_id     = grafana_organization.lab_org.id
}
EOF
Subtask 3.3: Apply Data Source Configuration
Apply the Terraform configuration to create data sources:

# Plan the changes
terraform plan

# Apply the configuration
terraform apply -auto-approve

# Verify data sources were created
curl -u admin:admin123 http://localhost:3000/api/datasources
